What raylibz takes from raylib, and how:

* **The library itself.** raylibz does not vendor, patch or fork raylib. It
  depends on it, pinned by commit, and links the artifact raylib's own build
  script produces. raylib's `raylib.h`, `rcamera.h`, `raymath.h` and `rlgl.h`
  reach a raylibz user through raylib's own translation step, unmodified.

* **The names and declarations.** `src/enums.zig`, `src/types.zig`,
  `src/vector2.zig`, `src/vector3.zig`, `src/vector4.zig`, `src/matrix.zig` and
  `tests/functions.zig` are derived from `raylib.h` and `raymath.h`: every
  struct, field, enum, constant value, colour and function name is raylib's, and
  each mirror asserts that it is at compile time. The one-line documentation
  comments on the wrappers are raylib's own comments from `raylib.h`.

* **The example.** `examples/basic_window.zig` is a port of raylib's
  `examples/core/core_basic_window.c` and keeps raylib's example header notice,
  including its copyright line, at the top of the file.

The fork
--------

raylibz pins this raylib fork, by commit (Live at Head: nothing is tagged):

    https://github.com/mattneel/raylib
    branch zigpp, commit f0e501203d18f9c254fbabca353d08b8f4c698ac

It is raysan5/raylib's `master` at `fafa2cea97` (RAYLIB_VERSION "6.1-dev",
432 commits past the 6.0 tag) with one change: raylib's `build.zig` and
`build.zig.zon` are ported to Zig++'s `std.Build` API, so that raylib builds
with Zig++ and publishes the translations of its headers as the modules `raylib`,
`rcamera`, `raymath` and `rlgl`. raylib's C sources and public headers are
unchanged, and the fork's `build.zig` carries its own "ALTERED FOR ZIG++" note
saying so. raylibz's own dependency entry names the commit tarball and its hash;
`tests/functions.zig` records the same commit, so a bump is a visible, reviewed
change.

raylib-zig
----------

raylibz's shape owes a great deal to raylib-zig by Nikolas Wipper (Not-Nik) and
its contributors, MIT-licensed: https://github.com/raylib-zig/raylib-zig. It came
first, and it does most of this well. raylibz differs in where the C
declarations come from: raylib-zig maintains its own generated extern
declarations, while raylibz keeps none and wraps raylib's own translation,
proving the wrapper against it at compile time.
